{"type": "sena", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Programming Languages: C, C++ and C#"}, {"type": "position", "content": 5}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear and use when talking about C, C++ and other programming languages."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe a general-purpose computer programming language, regarding its history and architecture in spoken and written situations."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on principles, concepts, and features of general purpose computer programming languages."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n5le01ob05re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The Future With Will, Going to, the Present Continuous and the Present Simple"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "When we want to talk about the future, there are many options; we can use <strong>will</strong>, <strong>going to</strong>, <strong>present continuous</strong> and <strong>present simple</strong>, each one in a particular context or situation."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Many other languages only interact with C, and I think your code will consist of little more than the interface."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Besides, since its object-oriented programming is not built in, it is going to give you more freedom to set up structures to act like objects."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Well, when is it due?  "}, {"type": "example", "content": "That's exactly what I'm doing. I think I'll work on that all weekend long."}, {"type": "text", "content": "The <strong>present simple</strong> is used to talk about future events that are clear because they're fixed on a schedule or timetable:<br/><br/>  Subject + Verb (-s or -es with he/she/it) + Complement<br/><br/>  Example:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">The programming course <strong>starts</strong> next Tuesday at 10:00 a.m.</span><br/><br/>  The <strong>present continuous</strong> is used to talk about arrangements for events (plans) at a time later than now. It is normally used when there is a very high degree of probability due to a near future situation:<br/><br/>  Subject + am/is/are+ Verb (ing) + Complement<br/><br/>  Example:<br/>  <span class=\"italic\">She <strong>is finishing</strong> the program set up tomorrow night.</span><br/><br/>  <strong>Will</strong> is used in different situations; when we refer to rapid decisions, offers, promises or quick thoughts at the moment of speaking.<br/><br/>  Subject + Will + Verb + Complement<br/><br/> Example:<br/>  <span class=\"italic\">I'<strong>ll</strong> start the computer while you finish reading that.</span><br/><br/>  <strong>Will</strong> is also used to make predictions without evidence:<br/><br/>  Example: <span class=\"italic\">Finishing this programming <strong>will</strong> be difficult.</span><br/><br/>  <strong>Be going to</strong> is used to talk about plans or decisions made before the moment of speaking, or when there is an intention of doing something:<br/><br/>  Subject + Going to + Verb + Complement<br/><br/> Example:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">I'm <strong>going to</strong> use the C++ programming language for the development of this app.</span><br/><br/>  If we want to make predictions based on some kind of evidence, we also use <strong>be going to</strong>:<br/><br/> Example:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">She's really good at this. By the end of the day, the program <storng>is going to</strong> be finished.</span>  "}, {"type": "example", "content": "My next class starts in 5 minutes."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Have Got to and Going to Have to for What Is Necessary"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<strong>Have got to</strong> and <strong>going to have to</strong> are used to express an obligation that, for external circumstances, is necessary. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "These modal verbs work in different tenses: present and future. In the present simple tense, use <strong>have got to</strong> for <span class=\"italic\">I, you, we, and they</span>; and <strong>has got to</strong> for <span class=\"italic\">he, she and it.</span><br/><br/> Example:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">Karla <strong>has got to</strong> finish that programming before noon.</span><br/><br/>  To form these sentences in the future tense, write <span class=\"italic\"><strong>going to have to</strong></span> after the subject and verb <strong>to be</strong>: <br/><br/> Example:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">You're <strong>going to have to</strong> work all day on that website.</span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I've got to design my own application using C or C++ programming languages, and I don't even know where to start."}, {"type": "example", "content": "We've got to present an update next Monday. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "You're going to have to."}, {"type": "example", "content": "But I don't think you have got to use this language, right? "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Anyway, I've got to go now!"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "built-in  "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "incorporated as an integral part "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Besides, since its object-oriented programming is not built-in, it is going to give you more freedom to setup structures to act like objects."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le01ob05aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "general-purpose "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "useful in many ways; not limited to one use or function"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: First of all, C is a general-purpose programming language with object-oriented features."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le01ob05aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "lexical scope "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "part of source code in which a binding of a name with an entity applies"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: It supports structured programming, lexical variable scope and recursion."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le01ob05aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "machine instructions "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "many bytes in memory that indicate the processor to perform a particular machine operation"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I think you should work on some sort of app for machine instructions. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le01ob05aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "recursion"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "method where the resolution to a problem is determined by solutions to smaller samples of the same problem"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: It supports structured programming, lexical variable scope, and recursion."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le01ob05aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "scalar"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "atomic amount that is able to contain only one value at a time"}, {"type": "part_speech", "